-- MAGIC         };
-- MAGIC     });
-- MAGIC })();
-- MAGIC </script>

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### C3. Create a Policy
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Now let's create a policy that redacts values based on classification tags.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Policies are inherited downward like privileges, so they can be created on a table, schema or catalog; whichever makes sense given the context. In this case, we'll apply it on the schema so the policy will apply on all contained tables.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Navigate to the `traffic` schema (link provided in the setup output at the beginning of this notebook).
-- MAGIC 1. In the **Policies** tab, click **New policy**.
-- MAGIC 1. Provide a **Name** for the policy.
-- MAGIC 1. In the **Applied to** field, select *public_app*.
-- MAGIC 1. Observe the **Scope**, which will be *law_prd*, *traffic* and *All tables*, respectively, because the policy was created on the *traffic* schema.
-- MAGIC 1. In the **Purpose** area, select **Mask column data**.
-- MAGIC 1. In the **Conditions** area, select **Mask column if it has a specific tag**.
-- MAGIC 1. Using the dropdown, select all system classification tags (all tags beginning with *class.*).
-- MAGIC 1. The UI assists in building a function to support the policy, and provides templates for various use cases. Select **Mask with asterisks**.
-- MAGIC 1. Click **Validate function**. Test the function if desired.
-- MAGIC 1. Click **Create policy**.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In addition to creating the policy, it creates a function in the schema; in this case `mask_value()`.

-- COMMAND ----------

